% ============================================================================
% compound-word-spelling-example — compound words used as a beginner
% multisyllable-spelling teaching example (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% The SIXTH literacy sub-skill library in this loop's sweep, and the first
% to move BEYOND CCSS RF.K.2 (rhyming/syllables/onset-rime/initial-sound/
% phoneme-substitution, all five named parts of which are now covered by
% `word-families.adj`/`syllable-count.adj`/`onset-rime.adj`/
% `initial-sound.adj`/`phoneme-substitution.adj`). This one grounds a
% SPELLING pattern, not a phonological-awareness one: teaching a beginner to
% spell multisyllable words is easier when the word is a compound built from
% two words the learner can already spell. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? compound_word_spelling_example(catfish, $Use)   % beginner_multisyllable_spelling
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `compound_word_spelling_example(word, teaching_use)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the teaching use WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on any word the source does not name. The query
% also runs BACKWARD — bind the teaching use and recall every word it
% applies to:
%
%     ? compound_word_spelling_example($W, beginner_multisyllable_spelling)
%       % catfish, hotdog, playground, yellowtail
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why this table has a second, constant-per-row column instead
% of a bare `columns word`: an earlier draft used a single-column
% `columns word` shape and found (empirically, in a scratch dir, before
% writing this file) that a fully-ground single-column query does NOT
% produce ordinary `recall`/`abstained` semantics — the engine instead falls
% back to its hypothesis-ranking/adjudication mode (`ranked`/`decision`/
% `kickback` fields), which is the wrong shape for a plain citable fact.
% Giving every table AT LEAST two genuine columns, even when the second
% column is the same constant label for every row (as here), keeps the
% query shape the ordinary recall one every other table in this stdlib uses.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Reading Rockets' "How Spelling Supports
% Reading" article, which names catfish, hotdog, playground, and yellowtail
% as the SAME four example words to begin multisyllable-spelling instruction
% with. WebFetch-verified TWICE for consistency before writing.
% `trust consensus` — a widely-used K-12 literacy resource site, the same
% tier as the other Reading Rockets citations already shipped in this
% directory (`word-families.adj`, `onset-rime.adj`). A word that is NOT one
% of these four — e.g. `cupcake`, also a real compound word but not named by
% this source — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than inventing a teaching use the source never asserted. This library
% intentionally does NOT cite a specific CCSS standard code: the closest
% candidates (RF.1.3.e general phonics, L.2.4.d compound-word MEANING
% prediction) both describe a DIFFERENT skill than what this source actually
% supports (spelling EASE via compound decomposition, not decoding or
% meaning) — asserting either would outrun the citation, so `standards`
% stays empty, matching the discipline of not force-citing a standard the
% source does not itself ground. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table compound_word_spelling_example {
    columns word, teaching_use

    row (catfish, beginner_multisyllable_spelling)
    row (hotdog, beginner_multisyllable_spelling)
    row (playground, beginner_multisyllable_spelling)
    row (yellowtail, beginner_multisyllable_spelling)

    source "To teach how to spell multisyllable words, consider beginning with compounds (catfish, hotdog, playground, and yellowtail)."
    locator "https://www.readingrockets.org/topics/early-literacy-development/articles/how-spelling-supports-reading"
    trust consensus
}
